SET 2 — ANIMATIONS 16–30
==================================================


16 — CARD ZOOM IN

TYPE:
Hover / Focus

On hover or selection:

scale: 1 → approximately 1.06
translateY: 0 → approximately -8px
translateZ increases slightly

Strengthen the shadow simultaneously.

Neighboring cards can reduce scale/opacity very slightly.

Transition should feel controlled and premium.

Do not create an aggressive zoom.


17 — SLIDE REVEAL

TYPE:
Reveal / Interactive

Create layered cards where content is initially partially hidden.

Interaction causes:

foreground layer → slides sideways

revealing:

secondary content/image/details underneath.

Use clipping/masking where appropriate.

Animation:

COVER
→ SLIDE
→ REVEAL CONTENT

Reverse cleanly when interaction ends.


18 — BLUR TO FOCUS

TYPE:
Depth / Focus

Center/selected card becomes visually sharp while surrounding cards lose emphasis.

ACTIVE:

blur: 0
scale: 1
opacity: 1

DISTANT:

small controlled blur
reduced scale
lower opacity

During navigation:

incoming card gradually sharpens while moving toward center.

Outgoing card gradually softens.

Keep blur subtle because filter animation can be expensive.


19 — SPLIT CARD

TYPE:
Creative Reveal

Visually divide the element into two synchronized halves.

On interaction:

LEFT HALF
→ shifts/rotates left

RIGHT HALF
→ shifts/rotates right

revealing secondary content beneath.

Both halves must originate from the same card and remain visually aligned before animation.

Reverse seamlessly.


20 — FLIP STACK

TYPE:
3D Stack

Combine deck stacking with 3D flipping.

Front card:

rotateY or rotateX
→ moves outward
→ travels behind stack

Remaining stack:

moves forward one level.

The returning card joins the rear.

Create continuous deck-like cycling.


21 — ROTATE & SCALE

TYPE:
Interactive Transform

Interaction combines:

rotation
scale
translation

Example:

rotate: 0 → 3–6deg
scale: 1 → 1.04
translateY: 0 → -6px

Direction can depend on pointer position.

Use springs.

The effect should be noticeably more dynamic than ordinary hover scaling while remaining elegant.


22 — MORPH TRANSITION

TYPE:
Creative / Shared Layout

One card smoothly transforms into another visual state.

Animate shared geometry:

position
scale
border radius
image area
content placement

For Framer Motion implementations prefer:

layout
layoutId

where appropriate.

The transformation should look continuous rather than:

old card disappears
→ new card appears.

Preserve visual continuity between states.


23 — ELASTIC BOUNCE

TYPE:
Spring / Playful

Cards move using carefully controlled elastic spring physics.

When selected:

card moves forward
→ slightly overshoots
→ settles naturally

Use spring configuration rather than keyframe fake-bouncing.

Example starting point:

type: spring
stiffness: 260
damping: 18
mass: 0.8

Tune values according to element size.

Avoid excessive repeated bouncing.


24 — PERSPECTIVE TILT

TYPE:
3D Pointer

Create stronger spatial tilt than Animation 07.

Pointer movement controls:

rotateX
rotateY
translateZ
perspective origin

The card should appear to exist on a physical plane in 3D space.

Optionally allow inner content to have separate depth levels:

background: z0
image: z20
text: z35
button: z45

This creates multi-layer parallax inside the tilted element.


25 — FADE & SLIDE

TYPE:
Minimal Transition

Create a clean sequential transition using:

opacity
translateX or translateY

Outgoing:

opacity 1 → 0
translate approximately 0 → -30px

Incoming:

opacity 0 → 1
translate approximately 30px → 0

Use elegant easing.

This animation should remain intentionally minimal and suitable for professional interfaces.


26 — CARD SHUFFLE

TYPE:
Dynamic Deck

Cards should behave like a shuffled physical deck.

On navigation:

active card moves sideways
→ rotates slightly
→ moves backward
→ joins rear of stack

Other cards advance.

Add small position/rotation offsets so the stack does not look mechanically identical.

Movement must remain deterministic enough to avoid visual chaos.


27 — SCROLL PARALLAX

TYPE:
Scroll / Depth

Unlike the basic vertical parallax example, tie multiple card layers directly to scroll progress.

Different cards use different movement ratios.

Example:

rear: scroll × 0.25
middle: scroll × 0.5
front: scroll × 0.8

Combine with subtle scale and depth.

For Framer Motion prompts use concepts such as:

useScroll
useTransform

Keep motion smooth and bounded.


28 — FLIP REVEAL CONTENT

TYPE:
3D Content Reveal

A single card has front and back states.

FRONT:
primary content

BACK:
secondary content

Interaction:

rotateY: 0 → 180deg

Use:

transform-style: preserve-3d
backface-visibility: hidden

Back side should be rotated 180deg initially.

Support click/tap, not only hover, so it works on mobile.


29 — GLOW FOCUS

TYPE:
Highlight / Focus

Selected card receives an animated focus glow.

The glow should react together with:

scale
depth
shadow
border intensity

Inactive cards become slightly less prominent.

The glow should feel like a premium highlight—not neon overload.

Avoid expensive giant animated blur filters.

Prefer pseudo-elements and opacity/transform where possible.


30 — CIRCLE CAROUSEL

TYPE:
Radial / 3D

Arrange cards around an invisible circular or elliptical path.

Each item position is derived from an angle.

Conceptually calculate:

x = radiusX × sin(angle)
z = radiusZ × cos(angle)

Optionally:

y = subtle vertical curve

Cards near the front:

larger
higher opacity
higher z-index

Cards near the rear:

smaller
lower opacity

Navigation rotates the entire logical carousel by one angular step.

Create the illusion of cards orbiting around a central axis.

